<Window x:Class="UefiAccess.App.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:res="clr-namespace:UefiAccess.Presentation.Resources;assembly=UefiAccess.Presentation"
        xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:UefiAccess.Presentation.ViewModels;assembly=UefiAccess.Presentation"
        xmlns:platform="clr-namespace:UefiAccess.App.Platform"
        xmlns:app="clr-namespace:UefiAccess.App"
        Title="{Binding WindowTitle, Mode=OneWay, FallbackValue={x:Static res:Strings.WindowTitle}}"
        AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding WindowTitle, Mode=OneWay, FallbackValue={x:Static res:Strings.WindowTitle}}"
        Width="1200"
        Height="800"
        MinWidth="900"
        MinHeight="600"
        FontSize="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemFonts.MessageFontSizeKey}}"
        Background="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlBrushKey}}"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlTextBrushKey}}"
        KeyboardNavigation.TabNavigation="Cycle"
        Loaded="OnLoaded"
        PreviewKeyDown="OnWindowPreviewKeyDown"
        PreviewKeyUp="OnWindowPreviewKeyUp"
        PreviewMouseUp="OnWindowPreviewMouseUp"
        Deactivated="OnDeactivated">
    <!--
        Alpha 1 shell (M2), with the Change group of M3. Stock controls only, no control templates,
        no timed behaviour in the markup (the one quiet period, of the detail pane's update, is the
        scheduler's; A2-7), every user-visible string from Presentation.Resources.Strings (the
        Writability sentences from Core's WritabilitySentences), colours only through SystemColors,
        and no hard-coded font size: every text follows SystemFonts.MessageFontSize, the metric the
        Windows "Text size" accessibility setting scales (the detail heading is that size scaled by
        a LayoutTransform, so it grows with it).

        Seven regions in F6 order since M4, each a named GroupBox (WPF on .NET 8 has no
        AutomationProperties.LandmarkType, so the regions are exposed as named groups):
        Toolbar, Filter, List, Detail, Log, Dell settings, Status. Tab order: toolbar buttons (1-5),
        filter box (6), Group by (7), Sort by (8), the variable list as one stop (9,
        KeyboardNavigation.TabNavigation Once on the ItemsControl of groups), the list/detail
        splitter (10), the detail heading (11), the detail fields (12, one read-only TextBox per
        field), the Writability line (13), the Change group (M3: the Boot order entries list 14,
        Move up 15, Move down 16, Review change 17, Boot once from this entry 18, Clear one-time
        boot 19, Restore previous value 20), the log splitter (21), the log list (22), Copy log (23),
        the Dell settings region (M4: Read Dell settings 24, the state line Dell settings source 25,
        the list Dell BIOS settings 26) and the Status box (27; 24 in the M3 builds, 17 before M3).

        The Dell settings region is a region of its own, after
        Log and before Status, because the Dell view is a second source that does not depend on the
        selected variable; it sits in an Auto row of the panes grid, so the log splitter and its
        30 percent are unchanged, and its list scrolls inside, its height capped by
        FitPanesToWindow. Its notes are on the region below.

        The Change group is a stock GroupBox inside the Detail
        region, after the Writability line, so it is no seventh region and F6 does not stop in it.
        It is shown only when the Presentation surface says so (Detail.Change.IsVisible: the live
        source, and BootOrder, BootNext or an active category-Boot Boot####), and inside it only the
        controls that belong to the variable shown are visible; a collapsed control is no tab stop.
        Nothing in it writes: Move up, Move down and Ctrl+Z rearrange the proposed order (one
        ObservableCollection.Move each, so a row keeps its container and its focus), and the four
        buttons that open the Confirm change dialog (ConfirmChangeWindow.xaml) only start the checks.
        The boot order list has its own item peers (a ListBox, not a fourth plain ItemsControl) and a
        keyed row style of its own; its position is the stock item peer's ("1 of 2"). Nothing here
        relies on a RangeValue, and the Status box stays the only live region.

        The variable list is an ItemsControl of groups: one stock Expander per group (its header,
        a focusable toggle button, is named "<label>, <n> items" through AutomationProperties.Name
        on the Expander) holding one ListView in GridView mode for the group's rows. Every row's
        AutomationProperties.Name is the row sentence (VariableItemViewModel.AccessibleName). A
        single ListView with a GroupStyle cannot give focusable, collapsible group headers without
        replacing the GroupItem template, which the rules forbid; the code-behind bridges the arrow
        keys between the groups and takes Home and End across them so the list reads as one tree
        (Page Up, Page Down and type-ahead are the stock ListView's and stay inside a group). The
        column widths are fixed (a GridView cannot size columns proportionally); what does not fit
        scrolls in the outer scroll viewer, by mouse or with Ctrl+Left and Ctrl+Right.

        Alpha 1 round 1 (the tester's findings A1-1, A1-3 and A1-4): the three plain ItemsControls of
        this window (the groups, the detail sections, the fields of a section) have no automation
        peer. WPF's own ItemsControl peer puts one "data item" element per item in front of the
        item's controls, named from the generated container (or, unnamed, from the item's
        ToString()), so NVDA read a group header three times (data item, grouping, data grid) and
        a section title or a field label twice. Nothing in XAML can remove or hide that element: a
        container cannot be made a non-control, non-content element from markup, and a container
        without a peer of its own is given a default one. So the application opts out of it with
        the documented runtime switch Switch.System.Windows.Controls.ItemsControlDoesNotSupportAutomation
        (Directory.Build.props, UefiAccess.App only): a plain ItemsControl then has no peer, and the
        Expander, the GroupBox and the label and value of a field inside the templates are exposed
        directly under the scroll viewer or group that holds them. ListBox, ListView and ComboBox
        have peers of their own and are not affected. The generated containers therefore carry no
        name, and the ItemContainerStyles that once named them are gone. The per-group ListView is
        named by the group label alone ("EFI global variable rows") and carries no help text: the
        header sentence is the Expander's, the list help is the List region's (A1-2).

        The two splitters are AnnouncingGridSplitter (Platform/AnnouncingGridSplitter.cs, findings
        A1-5 and A2-8): the stock GridSplitter with a peer derived from the stock
        GridSplitterAutomationPeer that adds the Value pattern, read-only, whose value is the
        splitter's position as a sentence naming both panes ("List 63 percent of the width, details
        37 percent", "Log panel 30 percent of the height, list and details 70 percent"; Presentation
        SplitterPosition), read on arrival. The share is a whole percent: the list's share of the
        list and the detail pane, the log panel's share of the list/detail row and the log panel;
        each arrow press moves the splitter by one percent. After every press, and at the end of a
        drag that changed the share, the splitter's Moved event reaches the shell through the
        code-behind, and the shell says the sentence in the Status box ("Limit reached: " before it
        when the press could not move the splitter); the splitter itself raises no value change.
        Kind names the sentence each splitter says. A control subclass with the stock template and
        drag, not a template override; the implicit splitter style below targets the subclass,
        because an implicit style applies to its exact type only.

        Layout (round 2): the Status region is pinned to the bottom of the window in a row of its
        own, so no resize, text size or display scale can push it off the window. Above it, the
        list/detail row and the log row share the height as star rows, so the stock splitter keeps
        both minimums (the log row can no longer grow past the window); the list/detail row keeps
        160 while there is room and gives way first when there is not, down to 80, and only then
        does the part above Status scroll (FitPanesToWindow). The window's start-up and minimum
        sizes are reduced to the work area (Presentation WindowFit).

        Low vision (round 2): colours only from SystemColors, through DynamicResource, so a switch
        to a contrast theme repaints at once. Focus is shown by style triggers, not by focus visuals
        (which WPF draws only after a key press): a thicker border on text boxes and buttons, a
        border in the Highlight colour on the focused row (HighlightText when that row is selected
        in a contrast theme, which fills it with Highlight), a dark outline on a selected row while
        the list does not have focus, and the Highlight colour on a focused splitter. The rows of the
        boot order list, and since M4 the rows of the list of Dell BIOS settings, carry the same
        three row triggers as the variable rows.

        The Status box is the only polite live region; the log list is Off; nothing is Assertive.
    -->
    <Window.Resources>
        <!--
            Buttons: spacing, and a border two pixels thick in the text colour while the button has
            keyboard focus. WPF draws its dotted focus rectangle only once a key has been pressed,
            so Refresh, focused when the window opens, showed no focus at all (the padding shrinks
            by the extra border, so the label does not move). A style trigger, no template.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="ToolbarButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,6" />
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,4" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="96" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlTextBrushKey}}" />
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,5" />
                </Trigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!--
            Every text box: a border three pixels thick while it has keyboard focus (the stock look
            only changes the colour of a one-pixel border in the default theme; a contrast theme
            makes it five pixels thick itself). The padding shrinks by the extra border, so the
            text does not move.
        -->
        <Style TargetType="TextBox">
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="3" />
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
                </Trigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!-- The filter box: the same focus border, with more room around its text. -->
        <Style x:Key="FilterBoxStyle" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBox}}">
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,3" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,1" />
                </Trigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!--
            Detail fields: read-only text boxes that expose the Value pattern. A single-line value
            wraps inside its box however long it is, so a low-vision reader at a high text size or
            zoom never scrolls a box sideways. NVDA reads a text box that gets focus only up to the
            end of the visual line at the caret, unless text is selected, which it reads whole
            ("selected" and the text): a wrapped value was heard up to its first visual line (round
            1: Boot0000's Device path read as "HD", Writability cut at "from this"). So the window
            selects a single-line value whole when keyboard focus arrives on its box from another
            element (OnDetailFieldGotKeyboardFocus, VariableDetailViewModel.SelectionOnKeyboardArrival);
            Up and Down then read it one visual line at a time from the top (StartLinesAtTheTop). A
            click places the caret as usual, and focus coming back to the same box from its context
            menu or from another window leaves a selection or caret position of the user's own as
            it is. NVDA reads a selection of 512 characters or more only as "selected N characters",
            so a value that long is not selected: the caret goes to its start, NVDA reads its first
            line on screen, and its help text (DetailFieldViewModel.HelpText, which is
            VariableDetailViewModel.HelpTextFor of the field, notified whenever it changes) gives
            its length and says that Down reads it line by line. A multi-line value (the hex dump, a
            decoded value with line breaks) keeps its own lines unwrapped in a monospace font,
            scrolls inside the box, and is read line by line from the first. A row change keeps the
            box of every field both rows have in the same order and replaces only its text (the note
            on SectionsControl), so the box keeps no undo history (IsUndoEnabled off): a kept box
            would otherwise hold the values of the rows it showed before, and nothing in a read-only
            box can be undone anyway.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="DetailFieldStyle" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBox}}">
            <Setter Property="IsReadOnly" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="IsReadOnlyCaretVisible" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="IsUndoEnabled" Value="False" />
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
            <Setter Property="VerticalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Auto" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled" />
            <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="320" />
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,4" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsMultiline}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="NoWrap" />
                    <Setter Property="HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Auto" />
                    <Setter Property="AcceptsReturn" Value="True" />
                    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Consolas, Courier New, Lucida Console" />
                    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="72" />
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!--
            The log lines: named by their sentence. Keyed, because the boot order list of the Change
            group has a row style of its own for the same item type (BootOrderRowStyle).
        -->
        <Style x:Key="LogLineStyle" TargetType="ListBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
        </Style>

        <!--
            The rows of the boot order list in the Change group (M3): each named "Boot0002, Windows
            Boot Manager" (with ", inactive" or ", hidden" when so), with no help text of its own (the
            list's is heard once, on arrival), and outlined exactly like the variable rows: a border
            two pixels thick, transparent until a trigger colours it; a selected row keeps a
            ControlDarkDark outline while the list does not have focus; the focused row is outlined
            in Highlight, or in HighlightText when it is also selected in a contrast theme. The
            position ("1 of 2") is the stock ListBoxItem peer's, so the name does not repeat it. No
            template is replaced; colours and font are the system's.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="BootOrderRowStyle" TargetType="ListBoxItem">
            <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <MultiTrigger>
                    <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Property="IsSelected" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Property="Selector.IsSelectionActive" Value="False" />
                    </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlDarkDarkBrushKey}}" />
                </MultiTrigger>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightBrushKey}}" />
                </Trigger>
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding Path=(SystemParameters.HighContrast)}" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding IsKeyboardFocused, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding IsSelected, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightTextBrushKey}}" />
                </MultiDataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!--
            The rows of the list of Dell BIOS settings: keyed, and not
            based on the variable rows' style. Each row is named by its sentence, the name first
            (DellSettingRowViewModel.AccessibleName, "Num Lock at boot: Enabled. Possible values:
            Disabled, Enabled."), and carries no item status (D3: no Dell row can change) and no
            help text (the list's help is heard once, on arrival). Outlined exactly like the
            variable rows: a border two pixels thick, transparent until a trigger colours it; a
            selected row keeps a ControlDarkDark outline while the list does not have focus; the
            focused row is outlined in Highlight, or in HighlightText when it is also selected in a
            contrast theme. The position ("1 of 10") is the stock ListViewItem peer's. No template
            is replaced; colours and font are the system's.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="DellSettingRowStyle" TargetType="ListViewItem">
            <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <MultiTrigger>
                    <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Property="IsSelected" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Property="Selector.IsSelectionActive" Value="False" />
                    </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlDarkDarkBrushKey}}" />
                </MultiTrigger>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightBrushKey}}" />
                </Trigger>
                <MultiDataTrigger>
                    <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding Path=(SystemParameters.HighContrast)}" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding IsKeyboardFocused, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                        <Condition Binding="{Binding IsSelected, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                    </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightTextBrushKey}}" />
                </MultiDataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>

        <!-- Grid column headers show the column names; sorting is done with the Sort by combo box, so the headers are not tab stops. -->
        <Style TargetType="GridViewColumnHeader">
            <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False" />
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,2" />
        </Style>

        <!--
            Group headers: the stock Expander of a contrast theme draws its header in WindowText on
            no background of its own, that is on the window's Control background; ControlText is
            the colour that goes with it (the pair the window itself uses), also in a contrast
            theme whose window and button colours were edited apart.
        -->
        <Style TargetType="Expander">
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlTextBrushKey}}" />
        </Style>

        <!--
            Splitters: ControlDarkDark on the window's Control background, at least 3:1 in the
            default theme (105 on 240 grey, 4.8:1) and in every contrast theme; with keyboard focus
            the Highlight colour framed in HighlightText, so the focused splitter differs from the
            others in every theme. Targets the subclass (see the note above).
        -->
        <Style TargetType="platform:AnnouncingGridSplitter">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlDarkDarkBrushKey}}" />
            <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="True" />
            <Setter Property="ShowsPreview" Value="False" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightBrushKey}}" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightTextBrushKey}}" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
                </Trigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
    </Window.Resources>

    <!--
        Two rows: everything above the Status region, then the Status region itself (an Auto row
        that always gets its full height, so it is never pushed off the window). When the window is
        too small for everything, the part above gives way: first the list/detail row (from 160
        down to 80, FitPanesToWindow), then that part scrolls; nothing is cut off, and the Status
        region stays in view.
    -->
    <Grid x:Name="LayoutRoot" Margin="8">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!--
            The regions above Status (five, and since M4 six with the Dell settings), in a scroll
            viewer that scrolls only when the window is too small for them at the current text size
            (for example 900 x 600 at 200 percent text): the grid is exactly as high as the viewer
            (Height bound to ViewportHeight) and
            never lower than its rows need (MinHeight, FitPanesToWindow), so the star rows share
            the height as usual, and a vertical scroll bar appears only when that minimum does not
            fit. Keyboard focus scrolls the focused control into view. The viewer is layout only:
            not focusable, and unnamed, so a screen reader passes over it as it does over any
            unnamed pane.
        -->
        <ScrollViewer x:Name="PanesScroller"
                      Grid.Row="0"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                      Focusable="False"
                      SizeChanged="OnLayoutPartSizeChanged">
            <Grid x:Name="PanesGrid" Height="{Binding ViewportHeight, ElementName=PanesScroller, Mode=OneWay}">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <!-- The list and the detail pane: 160 high whenever there is room (FitPanesToWindow lowers the minimum, down to 80, when there is not). -->
                    <RowDefinition x:Name="ListDetailRow" Height="7*" MinHeight="160" />
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <!--
                        The log row is resizable (the log splitter above it) and must never become shorter
                        than its group header plus the Copy log button, or the button is clipped at the
                        bottom: finding A1-7, the button's descenders cut off at 200 percent text size once
                        the row had been shrunk (the fixed minimum of 72 was right for 100 percent only).
                        The minimum follows the Toolbar region's actual height: an Auto row with the same
                        group header chrome and buttons of the same style, so at any text size it is exactly
                        what the Log region needs for its header, its padding and one button (both come to
                        header + button + 8). The log list shrinks first; the button never does. Like the
                        list/detail row it is a star row, so the two share the height (three tenths for the
                        log panel at first) and the stock splitter between them keeps both minimums: the
                        log panel cannot grow past the window any more (round 2).
                    -->
                    <RowDefinition x:Name="LogRow" Height="3*" MinHeight="{Binding ActualHeight, ElementName=ToolbarRegion, Mode=OneWay}" />
                    <!--
                        The Dell settings region (M4) sizes to its content: an Auto row, so FitPanesToWindow
                        counts it among the fixed rows and the log splitter's share (three tenths) is
                        unchanged. Its list is capped (WindowFit.DellListMaximum, a quarter of the panes'
                        height and never less than four lines) and scrolls inside, so the ten rows never
                        push the Status region down.
                    -->
                    <RowDefinition x:Name="DellRow" Height="Auto" />
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <!-- Region 1: Toolbar -->
                <GroupBox x:Name="ToolbarRegion"
                          Grid.Row="0"
                          Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionToolbar}"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionToolbar}"
                          Padding="6,4,6,0"
                          Margin="0,0,0,4"
                          SizeChanged="OnLayoutPartSizeChanged">
                    <!--
                        Access keys as screen readers speak them (round 2): WPF reports a button's access key
                        as the bare underlined letter ("R"), which does nothing on its own because the window
                        requires Alt (App.xaml.cs). Each button therefore sets AutomationProperties.AccessKey
                        and AcceleratorKey to "Alt+<letter>". WPF 8 answers an empty access key whenever
                        AutomationProperties.AccessKey is set (UIElementAutomationPeer.GetAccessKeyCore), so
                        today the bare letter is gone and the accelerator key carries "Alt+R"; NVDA speaks
                        the two together as the shortcut. The same on Copy log.
                    -->
                    <WrapPanel x:Name="ToolbarPanel" Orientation="Horizontal">
                        <Button x:Name="RefreshButton"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.RefreshLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RefreshName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.RefreshHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.RefreshAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.RefreshAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding RefreshCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="1" />
                        <Button x:Name="OpenBaselineButton"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.OpenBaselineLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.OpenBaselineName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.OpenBaselineHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.OpenBaselineAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.OpenBaselineAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding OpenBaselineCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="2" />
                        <Button x:Name="ClearFilterButton"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearFilterLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearFilterName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearFilterHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearFilterAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearFilterAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding ClearFilterCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="3" />
                        <Button x:Name="CopyValueButton"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyValueLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyValueName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyValueHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyValueAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyValueAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding CopyValueCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="4" />
                        <Button x:Name="ExportVisibleListButton"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.ExportVisibleListLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ExportVisibleListName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ExportVisibleListHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ExportVisibleListAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ExportVisibleListAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding ExportVisibleListCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="5" />
                    </WrapPanel>
                </GroupBox>

                <!-- Region 2: Filter -->
                <GroupBox x:Name="FilterRegion"
                          Grid.Row="1"
                          Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionFilter}"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionFilter}"
                          Padding="6,4"
                          Margin="0,0,0,4"
                          SizeChanged="OnLayoutPartSizeChanged">
                    <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" MinWidth="200" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                                   x:Name="FilterLabel"
                                   Text="{x:Static res:Strings.FilterName}"
                                   AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.FilterName}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Margin="0,0,8,0" />
                        <TextBox Grid.Column="1"
                                 x:Name="FilterBox"
                                 Text="{Binding List.FilterText, Mode=TwoWay, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}"
                                 AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.FilterName}"
                                 AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.FilterHelp}"
                                 AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding ElementName=FilterLabel}"
                                 IsEnabled="{Binding IsFilterEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                                 TabIndex="6"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 Style="{StaticResource FilterBoxStyle}"
                                 TextChanged="OnFilterTextChanged"
                                 PreviewKeyDown="OnFilterPreviewKeyDown" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                                   x:Name="GroupByLabel"
                                   Text="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByName}"
                                   AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByName}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Margin="16,0,8,0" />
                        <ComboBox Grid.Column="3"
                                  x:Name="GroupByBox"
                                  SelectedValuePath="Tag"
                                  SelectedValue="{Binding List.GroupBy, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByName}"
                                  AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByHelp}"
                                  AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding ElementName=GroupByLabel}"
                                  IsEnabled="{Binding IsFilterEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  TabIndex="7"
                                  MinWidth="130"
                                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  SelectionChanged="OnGroupOrSortChanged">
                            <!--
                                Each item says where it stands in its list (A2-4): the stock peer of a
                                ComboBox's dropdown item gives no PositionInSet/SizeOfSet, so a screen
                                reader could not say "1 of 3" and Axe.Windows reports an error.
                            -->
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByNamespace}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByNamespace}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="1" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="3" Tag="{x:Static vm:GroupBy.Namespace}" />
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByKind}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByKind}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="2" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="3" Tag="{x:Static vm:GroupBy.Kind}" />
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByNone}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupByNone}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="3" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="3" Tag="{x:Static vm:GroupBy.None}" />
                        </ComboBox>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="4"
                                   x:Name="SortByLabel"
                                   Text="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByName}"
                                   AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByName}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Margin="16,0,8,0" />
                        <ComboBox Grid.Column="5"
                                  x:Name="SortByBox"
                                  SelectedValuePath="Tag"
                                  SelectedValue="{Binding List.SortBy, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByName}"
                                  AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByHelp}"
                                  AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding ElementName=SortByLabel}"
                                  IsEnabled="{Binding IsFilterEnabled, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  TabIndex="8"
                                  MinWidth="130"
                                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  SelectionChanged="OnGroupOrSortChanged">
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByNameOption}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByNameOption}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="1" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="4" Tag="{x:Static vm:SortBy.Name}" />
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByNamespace}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByNamespace}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="2" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="4" Tag="{x:Static vm:SortBy.Namespace}" />
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.SortBySize}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortBySize}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="3" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="4" Tag="{x:Static vm:SortBy.Size}" />
                            <ComboBoxItem Content="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByKind}" AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.SortByKind}" AutomationProperties.PositionInSet="4" AutomationProperties.SizeOfSet="4" Tag="{x:Static vm:SortBy.Kind}" />
                        </ComboBox>
                    </Grid>
                </GroupBox>

                <!-- Regions 3 and 4: List and Detail, side by side with a splitter -->
                <Grid Grid.Row="2">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="5*" MinWidth="280" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="3*" MinWidth="280" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <GroupBox x:Name="ListRegion"
                              Grid.Column="0"
                              Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionList}"
                              AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionList}"
                              AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.VariableListHelp}"
                              Focusable="True"
                              IsTabStop="False"
                              Padding="4">
                        <ScrollViewer x:Name="ListScroller"
                                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                      CanContentScroll="False"
                                      Focusable="False"
                                      AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.VariableListName}">
                            <ItemsControl x:Name="GroupsControl"
                                          ItemsSource="{Binding List.Groups}"
                                          KeyboardNavigation.TabNavigation="Once"
                                          KeyboardNavigation.DirectionalNavigation="None"
                                          TabIndex="9"
                                          Focusable="False"
                                          PreviewKeyDown="OnGroupsPreviewKeyDown"
                                          PreviewMouseDown="OnGroupsPreviewMouseDown">
                                <!--
                                    No ItemContainerStyle: the generated containers are not automation elements
                                    (the ItemsControlDoesNotSupportAutomation note at the top), so nothing names them
                                    and the Expander below is the first element a screen reader meets for a group.
                                -->
                                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                    <DataTemplate>
                                        <!--
                                            Keyboard focus lands on the expander's header toggle button (a part of the stock
                                            template), not on the expander itself, so the Loaded handler copies the help text
                                            onto that button (OnGroupHeaderLoaded) and the expander keeps it as well.
                                            NVDA also speaks the expander itself (its name, "grouping", "expanded" and this
                                            help text) and then the group's grid ("<label> rows", "data grid") whenever focus
                                            enters them from outside, before the row: the group-edge speech that a quick next
                                            key cuts before the row's name (finding A2-7, re-analysis of 3 October 2026).
                                        -->
                                        <Expander IsExpanded="{Binding IsExpanded, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                  Header="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                  AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                  AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.GroupHeaderHelp}"
                                                  TabIndex="9"
                                                  Margin="0,0,0,4"
                                                  Loaded="OnGroupHeaderLoaded">
                                            <!--
                                                The rows grid is named by the group label alone ("EFI global variable rows",
                                                VariableGroupViewModel.RowsAccessibleName), never by the header sentence the
                                                Expander has just spoken, and carries no help text: the list help is the List
                                                region's and would otherwise be heard again on every entry into a group.
                                            -->
                                            <ListView ItemsSource="{Binding Items}"
                                                      SelectionMode="Single"
                                                      SelectedItem="{Binding DataContext.List.SelectedItem, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=Window}, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                      AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding RowsAccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                      TabIndex="9"
                                                      IsTextSearchEnabled="True"
                                                      TextSearch.TextPath="Name"
                                                      ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                      ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                      BorderThickness="0"
                                                      Margin="12,0,0,0"
                                                      SelectionChanged="OnGroupListSelectionChanged">
                                                <ListView.ItemsPanel>
                                                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                                        <VirtualizingStackPanel Orientation="Vertical" />
                                                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                                </ListView.ItemsPanel>
                                                <ListView.ItemContainerStyle>
                                                    <Style TargetType="ListViewItem">
                                                        <!-- No HelpText on the rows: a screen reader that reads help text on focus would repeat it after every row sentence; the list's help text is on the List region, spoken once on arrival. -->
                                                        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" />
                                                        <!--
                                                            M3 (deviation D3): the row's item status, "Changeable: order of entries" on
                                                            BootOrder and "Changeable: one-time boot" on BootNext while they are
                                                            changeable, "Not changeable on this machine" (since 7 October 2026) on both on a
                                                            machine that is not the M3 test unit, empty on every other row
                                                            (VariableItemViewModel.ItemStatus, from Core's WritabilityPolicy). What a
                                                            screen reader speaks for it is not known, so those two rows carry the same
                                                            words in their sentence as well.
                                                        -->
                                                        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.ItemStatus" Value="{Binding ItemStatus, Mode=OneWay}" />
                                                        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
                                                        <!--
                                                            Low vision (round 2): every row has a border two pixels thick, transparent
                                                            until one of the triggers below colours it, so nothing moves when it does.
                                                            Style triggers outrank the triggers of the stock row template, so no
                                                            template is replaced. A selected row keeps a ControlDarkDark outline while
                                                            the list does not have focus (the stock default theme greys it out to
                                                            almost nothing, 1.2 to 1.6:1); the focused row is outlined in Highlight,
                                                            or, when it is also selected in a contrast theme (where every selected row
                                                            is already filled with Highlight, focused or not), in HighlightText. A
                                                            focused row that is not selected (after Ctrl+Up or Ctrl+Down) keeps the
                                                            Highlight outline in a contrast theme too: it sits on the Window colour,
                                                            where HighlightText can be the same black. The stock focus rectangle is
                                                            drawn only after a key press, and in a contrast theme in a colour that
                                                            nearly vanishes on Highlight (1.5 to 2.2:1).
                                                        -->
                                                        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
                                                        <Style.Triggers>
                                                            <MultiTrigger>
                                                                <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                                                    <Condition Property="IsSelected" Value="True" />
                                                                    <Condition Property="Selector.IsSelectionActive" Value="False" />
                                                                </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                                                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.ControlDarkDarkBrushKey}}" />
                                                            </MultiTrigger>
                                                            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                                                                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightBrushKey}}" />
                                                            </Trigger>
                                                            <MultiDataTrigger>
                                                                <MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                                                                    <Condition Binding="{Binding Path=(SystemParameters.HighContrast)}" Value="True" />
                                                                    <Condition Binding="{Binding IsKeyboardFocused, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                                                                    <Condition Binding="{Binding IsSelected, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}" Value="True" />
                                                                </MultiDataTrigger.Conditions>
                                                                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource {x:Static SystemColors.HighlightTextBrushKey}}" />
                                                            </MultiDataTrigger>
                                                        </Style.Triggers>
                                                    </Style>
                                                </ListView.ItemContainerStyle>
                                                <ListView.View>
                                                    <GridView AllowsColumnReorder="False">
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnName}" Width="150">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Name, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnNamespace}" Width="170">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding NamespaceLabel, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding NamespaceLabel, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnFlags}" Width="90">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Flags, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Flags, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnSize}" Width="70">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding SizeText, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding SizeText, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnKind}" Width="100">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Kind, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Kind, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                        <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnSummary}" Width="240">
                                                            <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                                <DataTemplate>
                                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Summary, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Summary, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                </DataTemplate>
                                                            </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                                        </GridViewColumn>
                                                    </GridView>
                                                </ListView.View>
                                            </ListView>
                                        </Expander>
                                    </DataTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl>
                        </ScrollViewer>
                    </GroupBox>

                    <!-- Ten pixels wide: a mouse target a low-vision user can hit (round 2; it was six). -->
                    <platform:AnnouncingGridSplitter x:Name="ListDetailSplitter"
                                                     Grid.Column="1"
                                                     Width="10"
                                                     HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                                     VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
                                                     ResizeDirection="Columns"
                                                     ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext"
                                                     Kind="ListAndDetails"
                                                     AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ListDetailSplitterName}"
                                                     AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ListDetailSplitterHelp}"
                                                     TabIndex="10"
                                                     Margin="2,0" />

                    <!-- Any entry of keyboard focus into the pane runs the pending detail refresh first (finding A1-6; the note on SectionsControl). -->
                    <GroupBox x:Name="DetailRegion"
                              Grid.Column="2"
                              Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionDetail}"
                              AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionDetail}"
                              Padding="4"
                              PreviewGotKeyboardFocus="OnDetailPreviewGotKeyboardFocus">
                        <ScrollViewer x:Name="DetailPane"
                                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                      Focusable="False"
                                      AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.DetailPaneName}"
                                      AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.DetailPaneHelp}"
                                      PreviewKeyDown="OnDetailPreviewKeyDown">
                            <StackPanel Margin="4">
                                <!-- The heading is the system font size scaled by a quarter (a LayoutTransform, so it follows the Windows text size like everything else). -->
                                <TextBlock x:Name="DetailHeading"
                                           Text="{Binding Detail.Heading, Mode=OneWay}"
                                           AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Detail.Heading, Mode=OneWay}"
                                           AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.DetailPaneHelp}"
                                           AutomationProperties.HeadingLevel="Level1"
                                           Focusable="True"
                                           KeyboardNavigation.TabIndex="11"
                                           FontWeight="Bold"
                                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                           Margin="0,0,0,6">
                                    <TextBlock.LayoutTransform>
                                        <ScaleTransform ScaleX="1.25" ScaleY="1.25" />
                                    </TextBlock.LayoutTransform>
                                </TextBlock>
                                <ItemsControl x:Name="SectionsControl"
                                              ItemsSource="{Binding Detail.Sections, Mode=OneWay}"
                                              Focusable="False"
                                              GotKeyboardFocus="OnDetailFieldGotKeyboardFocus"
                                              LostKeyboardFocus="OnDetailFieldLostKeyboardFocus">
                                    <!--
                                        With no variable selected this control has no items and therefore no size. It
                                        was collapsed when it still had an automation peer: an on-screen element with a
                                        null bounding rectangle is an Accessibility Insights failure (rule
                                        BoundingRectangleNotNull), the only one in the idle window. Under the
                                        ItemsControlDoesNotSupportAutomation switch (the note at the top) a plain
                                        ItemsControl has no peer at all, so the collapse is now a safeguard in case the
                                        switch is ever removed, and it keeps the empty control out of the layout. The
                                        heading above already reads "No variable selected", so collapsing costs nothing.
                                        A DataTrigger on the control's own HasItems: a style trigger like the one
                                        DetailFieldStyle already uses, not a control template - the stock template is
                                        untouched.
                                    -->
                                    <ItemsControl.Style>
                                        <Style TargetType="ItemsControl">
                                            <Style.Triggers>
                                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding HasItems, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}, Mode=OneWay}" Value="False">
                                                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                                                </DataTrigger>
                                            </Style.Triggers>
                                        </Style>
                                    </ItemsControl.Style>
                                    <!--
                                        Finding A1-6: the detail pane is not updated inside the key press that changed the
                                        selection. The shell hands the update to Platform/DispatcherDetailScheduler.cs (the
                                        CoalescingDetailScheduler policy over the dispatcher): the window holds the update
                                        from the moment a key or a mouse button goes down in the variable list until it comes
                                        up (GroupsControl PreviewKeyDown and PreviewMouseDown hold; the window's PreviewKeyUp,
                                        PreviewMouseUp and Deactivated release), and the release starts a quiet period of 300
                                        ms (Presentation DetailRefreshTiming.QuietPeriod, the scheduler's one timer): only when
                                        it passes with no key or button going down in the list is one Dispatcher.BeginInvoke
                                        posted at Background priority (served only when no input is waiting) that runs the
                                        newest refresh. Alpha 2 finding A2-7 (the tester's A2-5): separate quick presses, 150 to 250
                                        ms apart, each cost an update right after the key came up while NVDA was still reading
                                        the row; now a burst of them costs one, after the last. Nothing the person asks for
                                        waits: every entry of keyboard focus into this pane runs the pending refresh first
                                        (FocusFirstDetailField for Enter, Space and F6; OnDetailPreviewGotKeyboardFocus on
                                        DetailRegion for Tab, a click and a screen reader's focus request), so focus never
                                        lands in stale details; an Alt key anywhere in the window runs it too
                                        (OnWindowPreviewKeyDown), so every access key, the Change group's among them, acts on
                                        the selected row, and so do Copy value, Export visible list and every Change command
                                        themselves (ShellViewModel.RunChangeAsync).
                                        The sections and the fields keep the stock StackPanel: a
                                        VirtualizingStackPanel virtualises only inside the scroll viewer of its own
                                        ItemsControl's template, which a plain ItemsControl has not (the scroll viewer here
                                        is the pane's), and even where it worked it would hide the fields that are not
                                        realised from Tab, from FocusFirstDetailField and from a screen reader walking the
                                        tree, so the cost of the pane is kept down by refreshing it less often instead.
                                        The update itself is made in place (the A16 measurement on the OptiPlex 7020: removing
                                        and re-creating every section and field raised about 80 StructureChanged and 70
                                        PropertyChanged UI Automation events, and the next row's focus event reached NVDA up
                                        to about 215 ms late). Detail.Sections and the Fields of each section are collections
                                        that are never replaced, cleared or reset: a row change keeps every section and field
                                        the two rows both have in the same order, with its group, label and box and their
                                        automation elements, changes only the box's text and help text (Value and HelpText,
                                        notifying properties), and inserts or removes, one at a time, only the sections and
                                        fields that differ.
                                        The window starts every kept box that does not have keyboard focus at its top again
                                        (caret and scroll position), as a new box started (OnDetailPropertyChanged).
                                    -->
                                    <!-- No ItemContainerStyle: the containers are not automation elements (note at the top); the GroupBox is the section's one element, named by its title. -->
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                        <DataTemplate>
                                            <GroupBox AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Title, Mode=OneWay}" Margin="0,0,0,6" Padding="4">
                                                <GroupBox.Header>
                                                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Title, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                               AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Title, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                               AutomationProperties.HeadingLevel="Level2"
                                                               FontWeight="SemiBold" />
                                                </GroupBox.Header>
                                                <!--
                                                    Label above value, each field the full width of the pane: a label as long as
                                                    EFI_VARIABLE_TIME_BASED_AUTHENTICATED_WRITE_ACCESS wraps instead of squeezing
                                                    the value box, at any text size. The label block is named by the label alone
                                                    (it shows nothing else); the value box is named by the label alone too
                                                    (DetailFieldViewModel.AccessibleName, "Raw data, hex dump" for the hex box)
                                                    and exposes the value through the Value pattern, so a screen reader arriving
                                                    on a field hears the label once and the value once, never the value twice.
                                                -->
                                                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Fields, Mode=OneWay}" Focusable="False">
                                                    <!-- No ItemContainerStyle: the containers are not automation elements (note at the top); a field is its label block and its value box, nothing in front of them. -->
                                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                                        <DataTemplate>
                                                            <StackPanel Margin="0,2,0,4">
                                                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                                           AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Label, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                                           Margin="0,0,0,2"
                                                                           TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                                <TextBox Text="{Binding Value, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                                         AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                                         AutomationProperties.HelpText="{Binding HelpText, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                                         AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding Header, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=GroupBox}}"
                                                                         Style="{StaticResource DetailFieldStyle}"
                                                                         TabIndex="12" />
                                                            </StackPanel>
                                                        </DataTemplate>
                                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                                </ItemsControl>
                                            </GroupBox>
                                        </DataTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                </ItemsControl>
                                <!--
                                    The Writability line: the selected variable's own sentence from Core's
                                    WritabilityPolicy (M3), "No variable selected" with nothing selected. Wraps and is
                                    selected whole when keyboard focus arrives, like every single-line field
                                    (DetailFieldStyle): NVDA reads it whole, to its last word.
                                -->
                                <TextBox x:Name="ReadOnlyStatusBox"
                                         Text="{Binding Detail.ReadOnlyStatus, Mode=OneWay, FallbackValue={x:Static res:Strings.NoSelectionHeading}}"
                                         AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadOnlyStatusName}"
                                         AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.DetailFieldHelp}"
                                         IsReadOnly="True"
                                         IsReadOnlyCaretVisible="True"
                                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                         TabIndex="13"
                                         Margin="0,4,0,0"
                                         GotKeyboardFocus="OnReadOnlyStatusGotKeyboardFocus"
                                         LostKeyboardFocus="OnReadOnlyStatusLostKeyboardFocus" />

                                <!--
                                    The Change group: collapsed unless the Presentation
                                    surface shows it, and inside it only the controls of the variable shown. For
                                    BootOrder: the Boot order entries list (the proposed order, a copy of the
                                    firmware's), Move up, Move down and Review change. For an active category-Boot
                                    Boot####: Boot once from this entry. For BootNext: Clear one-time boot. Restore
                                    previous value joins BootOrder's and BootNext's controls when a snapshot of that
                                    variable exists. Every button is a stock button with its label's access key
                                    (Alt+U, Alt+D, Alt+V, Alt+B, Alt+M, Alt+P, which work from anywhere in the window
                                    while the button is shown; the Alt key first brings the detail pane up to date,
                                    finding A2-7, so the buttons are those of the selected row), the same
                                    "Alt+<letter>" as the toolbar reports, and a
                                    command; none writes by itself: Review change, Boot once from this entry, Clear
                                    one-time boot and Restore previous value run the checks and then open the Confirm
                                    change dialog, and only that dialog's Confirm change writes. A command that would
                                    change nothing answers with a sentence instead of being disabled, because a
                                    disabled button would swallow its access key; every command is disabled only while
                                    the checks or a write run. The keys of the list that stock WPF does not give
                                    (Ctrl+Z, and Enter and Space doing nothing on a row) are the code-behind's
                                    (OnBootOrderListPreviewKeyDown); Escape anywhere in the group is the pane's rule,
                                    back to the variable list at the same row.
                                -->
                                <GroupBox x:Name="ChangeGroup"
                                          Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ChangeGroupName}"
                                          AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ChangeGroupName}"
                                          AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ChangeGroupHelp}"
                                          Visibility="{Binding Detail.Change.IsVisible, Mode=OneWay, Converter={x:Static app:MainWindow.VisibleWhenTrue}}"
                                          Margin="0,8,0,0"
                                          Padding="4">
                                    <StackPanel>
                                        <StackPanel x:Name="BootOrderControls"
                                                    Visibility="{Binding Detail.Change.ShowsBootOrderControls, Mode=OneWay, Converter={x:Static app:MainWindow.VisibleWhenTrue}}">
                                            <TextBlock x:Name="BootOrderEntriesLabel"
                                                       Text="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOrderEntriesName}"
                                                       AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOrderEntriesName}"
                                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                       Margin="0,0,0,2" />
                                            <!--
                                                One row per entry of the proposed order, single selection: Up and Down
                                                select, Ctrl+Up and Ctrl+Down move focus without selecting (stock), and a
                                                move rearranges the collection the list shows, so the moved row keeps its
                                                container; should WPF regenerate it, the window puts focus back on it
                                                (FocusTarget.BootOrderEntry). The list help is heard once, on arrival.
                                            -->
                                            <ListBox x:Name="BootOrderList"
                                                     ItemsSource="{Binding Detail.Change.Editor.Entries, Mode=OneWay}"
                                                     SelectedItem="{Binding Detail.Change.Editor.SelectedEntry, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                                     SelectionMode="Single"
                                                     ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource BootOrderRowStyle}"
                                                     AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOrderEntriesName}"
                                                     AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOrderEntriesHelp}"
                                                     AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding ElementName=BootOrderEntriesLabel}"
                                                     TabIndex="14"
                                                     ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                                     PreviewKeyDown="OnBootOrderListPreviewKeyDown">
                                                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                                    <DataTemplate>
                                                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                                    </DataTemplate>
                                                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                            </ListBox>
                                            <WrapPanel x:Name="BootOrderButtons" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,6,0,0">
                                                <Button x:Name="MoveUpButton"
                                                        Content="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveUpLabel}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveUpName}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveUpHelp}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveUpAccessKey}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveUpAccessKey}"
                                                        Command="{Binding Detail.Change.MoveUpCommand}"
                                                        Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                        TabIndex="15" />
                                                <Button x:Name="MoveDownButton"
                                                        Content="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveDownLabel}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveDownName}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveDownHelp}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveDownAccessKey}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.MoveDownAccessKey}"
                                                        Command="{Binding Detail.Change.MoveDownCommand}"
                                                        Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                        TabIndex="16" />
                                                <Button x:Name="ReviewChangeButton"
                                                        Content="{x:Static res:Strings.ReviewChangeLabel}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ReviewChangeName}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ReviewChangeHelp}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ReviewChangeAccessKey}"
                                                        AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ReviewChangeAccessKey}"
                                                        Command="{Binding Detail.Change.ReviewChangeCommand}"
                                                        Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                        TabIndex="17" />
                                            </WrapPanel>
                                        </StackPanel>
                                        <WrapPanel x:Name="OneTimeBootButtons" Orientation="Horizontal">
                                            <Button x:Name="BootOnceButton"
                                                    Content="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOnceLabel}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOnceName}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOnceHelp}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOnceAccessKey}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.BootOnceAccessKey}"
                                                    Command="{Binding Detail.Change.BootOnceCommand}"
                                                    Visibility="{Binding Detail.Change.ShowsBootOnce, Mode=OneWay, Converter={x:Static app:MainWindow.VisibleWhenTrue}}"
                                                    Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                    TabIndex="18" />
                                            <Button x:Name="ClearBootNextButton"
                                                    Content="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearBootNextLabel}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearBootNextName}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearBootNextHelp}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearBootNextAccessKey}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ClearBootNextAccessKey}"
                                                    Command="{Binding Detail.Change.ClearBootNextCommand}"
                                                    Visibility="{Binding Detail.Change.ShowsClearBootNext, Mode=OneWay, Converter={x:Static app:MainWindow.VisibleWhenTrue}}"
                                                    Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                    TabIndex="19" />
                                            <Button x:Name="RestorePreviousButton"
                                                    Content="{x:Static res:Strings.RestorePreviousLabel}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RestorePreviousName}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.RestorePreviousHelp}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.RestorePreviousAccessKey}"
                                                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.RestorePreviousAccessKey}"
                                                    Command="{Binding Detail.Change.RestorePreviousCommand}"
                                                    Visibility="{Binding Detail.Change.ShowsRestore, Mode=OneWay, Converter={x:Static app:MainWindow.VisibleWhenTrue}}"
                                                    Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                                    TabIndex="20" />
                                        </WrapPanel>
                                    </StackPanel>
                                </GroupBox>
                            </StackPanel>
                        </ScrollViewer>
                    </GroupBox>
                </Grid>

                <!-- The spoken share is the log panel's, the row after the splitter (TracksNextDefinition); ten pixels high, like the other splitter. -->
                <platform:AnnouncingGridSplitter x:Name="LogSplitter"
                                                 Grid.Row="3"
                                                 Height="10"
                                                 HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                 ResizeDirection="Rows"
                                                 ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext"
                                                 TracksNextDefinition="True"
                                                 Kind="LogPanel"
                                                 AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.LogSplitterName}"
                                                 AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.LogSplitterHelp}"
                                                 TabIndex="21"
                                                 Margin="0,2" />

                <!-- Region 5: Log -->
                <GroupBox x:Name="LogRegion"
                          Grid.Row="4"
                          Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionLog}"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionLog}"
                          Padding="4">
                    <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ListBox x:Name="LogList"
                                 Grid.Column="0"
                                 ItemsSource="{Binding Log.Lines}"
                                 SelectionMode="Single"
                                 AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.LogPanelName}"
                                 AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.LogPanelHelp}"
                                 AutomationProperties.LiveSetting="Off"
                                 ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource LogLineStyle}"
                                 TabIndex="22"
                                 ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                            <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                <DataTemplate>
                                    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding AccessibleName, Mode=OneWay}">
                                        <TextBlock.Text>
                                            <MultiBinding StringFormat="{}{0} {1} {2}">
                                                <Binding Path="Level" Mode="OneWay" />
                                                <Binding Path="Time" Mode="OneWay" />
                                                <Binding Path="Message" Mode="OneWay" />
                                            </MultiBinding>
                                        </TextBlock.Text>
                                    </TextBlock>
                                </DataTemplate>
                            </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                        </ListBox>
                        <!-- Sizes to its text: no Height, MinHeight or MaxHeight, and the log row above keeps room for it at any text size (the note on the row definitions). -->
                        <Button x:Name="CopyLogButton"
                                Grid.Column="1"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyLogLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyLogName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyLogHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyLogAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.CopyLogAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding CopyLogCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="23"
                                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                                Margin="8,0,0,0" />
                    </Grid>
                </GroupBox>

                <!--
                    Region 6: Dell settings. Shows a short, agreed list of
                    this computer's Dell BIOS settings and changes none of them. They are read only when Read
                    Dell settings (Alt+S) is pressed: never when the window opens, never by Refresh or Open
                    baseline folder, never on a timer; a baseline folder shows the settings it recorded.
                    Three stops, in document order, in a grid of three Auto rows: the Read Dell settings
                    button (24), never disabled (no IsEnabled binding; its command can always run, so Alt+S
                    always answers, also while a read runs, and focus is never lost under the button); the
                    state line Dell settings source (25), a read-only box that says when and where the
                    values were loaded, or why they could not be, selected whole when keyboard focus arrives
                    on it (the value boxes' arrival rule, OnDellSourceGotKeyboardFocus: without it NVDA reads
                    a wrapped value only to the end of its first line); and the list Dell BIOS settings (26),
                    a ListView in GridView mode, one tab stop, one row per agreed setting.
                    The rows exist from the start, the first one selected, and every read, baseline folder
                    or return to the live view changes their texts in place: no row is ever added, removed
                    or re-created (findings A1-6 and A2-7, a burst of UI Automation events delays the screen
                    reader), and nothing is collapsed or shown. The list is not virtualised, so all its rows
                    are always in the UI Automation tree, and it scrolls inside, its height capped by
                    FitPanesToWindow. A letter jumps to the next row whose name starts with it (stock text
                    search); Ctrl+C copies the row's sentence, and Enter and Space do nothing, there being no
                    detail pane (OnDellListPreviewKeyDown). The group has no help text (it would be heard on
                    every F6); its two keyboard focus handlers tell the shell which part has focus, so that
                    Copy value (Alt+Y) copies the Dell row or the state line there, never a firmware
                    variable's value. The Status box stays the only live region.
                -->
                <GroupBox x:Name="DellRegion"
                          Grid.Row="5"
                          Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionDell}"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionDell}"
                          Padding="6,4"
                          Margin="0,4,0,0"
                          SizeChanged="OnLayoutPartSizeChanged"
                          GotKeyboardFocus="OnDellRegionGotKeyboardFocus"
                          LostKeyboardFocus="OnDellRegionLostKeyboardFocus">
                    <Grid>
                        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                        <Button x:Name="ReadDellSettingsButton"
                                Grid.Row="0"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                Content="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadDellSettingsLabel}"
                                AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadDellSettingsName}"
                                AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadDellSettingsHelp}"
                                AutomationProperties.AccessKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadDellSettingsAccessKey}"
                                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="{x:Static res:Strings.ReadDellSettingsAccessKey}"
                                Command="{Binding ReadDellSettingsCommand}"
                                Style="{StaticResource ToolbarButtonStyle}"
                                TabIndex="24" />
                        <TextBox x:Name="DellSourceBox"
                                 Grid.Row="1"
                                 Text="{Binding Dell.StateText, Mode=OneWay}"
                                 AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.DellSourceName}"
                                 AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.DellSourceHelp}"
                                 IsReadOnly="True"
                                 IsReadOnlyCaretVisible="True"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                 TabIndex="25"
                                 Margin="0,0,0,4"
                                 GotKeyboardFocus="OnDellSourceGotKeyboardFocus"
                                 LostKeyboardFocus="OnDellSourceLostKeyboardFocus" />
                        <ListView x:Name="DellSettingsList"
                                  Grid.Row="2"
                                  ItemsSource="{Binding Dell.Rows, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  SelectedItem="{Binding Dell.SelectedRow, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  SelectionMode="Single"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.DellSettingsListName}"
                                  AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.DellSettingsListHelp}"
                                  ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource DellSettingRowStyle}"
                                  VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing="False"
                                  IsTextSearchEnabled="True"
                                  TextSearch.TextPath="DisplayName"
                                  TabIndex="26"
                                  PreviewKeyDown="OnDellListPreviewKeyDown">
                            <ListView.View>
                                <GridView AllowsColumnReorder="False">
                                    <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnDellSetting}" Width="220">
                                        <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate>
                                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding DisplayName, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding DisplayName, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                    </GridViewColumn>
                                    <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnDellCurrent}" Width="220">
                                        <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate>
                                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding CurrentText, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding CurrentText, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                    </GridViewColumn>
                                    <GridViewColumn Header="{x:Static res:Strings.ColumnDellPossible}" Width="420">
                                        <GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                            <DataTemplate>
                                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding PossibleText, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding PossibleText, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                                            </DataTemplate>
                                        </GridViewColumn.CellTemplate>
                                    </GridViewColumn>
                                </GridView>
                            </ListView.View>
                        </ListView>
                    </Grid>
                </GroupBox>
            </Grid>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <!-- Region 7: Status (the machine identity strip and the only polite live region), in the row of its own at the bottom -->
        <GroupBox x:Name="StatusRegion"
                  Grid.Row="1"
                  Header="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionStatus}"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Static res:Strings.RegionStatus}"
                  Padding="6,4"
                  Margin="0,4,0,0">
            <StackPanel>
                <WrapPanel x:Name="IdentityPanel" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding SourceDescription, Mode=OneWay, FallbackValue={x:Static res:Strings.LiveSourceName}}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding SourceDescription, Mode=OneWay, FallbackValue={x:Static res:Strings.LiveSourceName}}" FontWeight="SemiBold" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Manufacturer, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Manufacturer, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Model, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Model, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding BiosVersion, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding BiosVersion, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ServiceTag, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding ServiceTag, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Cpu, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Cpu, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding OperatingSystem, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding OperatingSystem, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ExpectedModelText, Mode=OneWay}" AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding ExpectedModelText, Mode=OneWay}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,16,2" />
                </WrapPanel>
                <TextBox x:Name="StatusBox"
                         Text="{Binding StatusText, Mode=OneWay}"
                         AutomationProperties.HelpText="{x:Static res:Strings.StatusHelp}"
                         AutomationProperties.LiveSetting="Polite"
                         IsReadOnly="True"
                         IsReadOnlyCaretVisible="True"
                         AcceptsReturn="True"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                         MinHeight="44"
                         MaxHeight="120"
                         TabIndex="27">
                    <!--
                        The spoken name carries the text on purpose: NVDA and Narrator speak a live region's
                        *name* when LiveRegionChanged is raised, and a TextBox's name does not follow its text
                        the way a TextBlock's does. Naming the box "Status" alone would make every announcement
                        say "Status" and nothing else. The price is that Tab or F6 onto the box reads the
                        message as the name and again as the value;
                        the detail fields, which are not live regions, are named by their labels alone.
                    -->
                    <AutomationProperties.Name>
                        <MultiBinding StringFormat="{}{0}: {1}">
                            <Binding Source="{x:Static res:Strings.StatusName}" />
                            <Binding Path="Text" RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Mode="OneWay" />
                        </MultiBinding>
                    </AutomationProperties.Name>
                </TextBox>
            </StackPanel>
        </GroupBox>
    </Grid>
</Window>
